[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · How should the shared validation module be loaded? → ES modules (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m ES modules. That also means [38;5;153mindex.html[39m needs to be opened over a local HTTP server rather than double-clicked — I'll
  note that in the README as part of the work.

  Next: [1mhow a form declares its rules[0m, which is the interface every future form gets locked into.

  - [1mRule arrays per field[0m — [38;5;153m{ username: [required()], password: [required(), minLength(8)] }[39m, where each rule is a small
    function returning an error message or nothing. Adding a rule type later is just writing another function; nothing
    central changes. Cross-field rules (confirm password) work because each rule receives the whole form's values
    alongside its own.
  - [1mFluent builder[0m — [38;5;153mfield('username').required().minLength(3)[39m. Reads nicely at the call site, but every rule has to be
    a method on the builder, so adding one means editing the builder class. More machinery for the same result.
  - [1mConfig object of rule names[0m — [38;5;153m{ username: { required: true, minLength: 3 } }[39m. Most compact and JSON-serializable,
    but rule names resolve through a central registry, so custom or cross-field rules are awkward to slot in.

  The declaration style is the part that's expensive to change later — it appears in every form. The rule [3mnames[0m and the
  error-message wording are cheap to change.

  I recommend [1mrule arrays[0m. It's the least machinery, custom rules need no framework buy-in, and the confirm-password
  case that signup needs falls out naturally instead of being a special case.
